    "narrative": "The LawDog offensive core is spearheaded today by **Bryce Harper** (68.07) and **Gleyber Torres** (67.45), both of whom offer the highest daily efficiency. **Bryce Harper** draws a favorable matchup against Wrobleski, and his high xwOBA puts him in a prime position to anchor our production. **Gleyber Torres** benefits significantly from a 13% boost provided by his dynamic platoon advantage against D. Peterson, combined with the offensive leverage of hitting at the top of the order. **Jung Hoo Lee** (65.94) remains a critical start, as his matchup against Avila allows him to exploit a soft SP_xERA of 4.96, while **Masyn Winn** (61.69) earns his spot not just for his efficiency, but for the rare +2% bonus tied to Ure\u00f1a\u2019s slow delivery, which provides a tangible lift in our quest for stolen bases.\n\nWe are navigating several complex platoon and matchup scenarios across the middle and bottom of the order. **Jonathan Aranda** (60.01) is a strong utilization play today; despite Gausman\u2019s 3.76 xERA, the dynamic platoon advantage against a right-handed arm propels him comfortably over our 60-point threshold. Conversely, the benching of **Lawrence Butler** (38.69) serves as our most notable \"near miss.\" While his base projection is solid, the combination of facing an elite arm like Drake and a restrictive dynamic platoon penalty pushed him below our 40-point \"Below Floor\" threshold, forcing us to prioritize the higher-ceiling utility of **Gavin Sheets** (55.81) and **Drew Gilbert** (55.45) in the outfield. \n\nWe must monitor the risks inherent in our bottom-tier starts. **Cal Raleigh** (47.95) and **Kyle Karros** (47.55) both face significant challenges from opposing pitchers; specifically, **Kyle Karros** faces a tough 3.05 xERA matchup, though he is salvaged by a substantial 14% park factor boost and the potential for aggressive base running against a slow-working staff. Similarly, **JJ Bleday** (46.11) is a marginal play who must overcome a -14% platoon drag and a difficult matchup against Castillo. As we navigate this slate, we remain cautious of these lower scores, benching players like **Yainer Diaz** and **Tyler Freeman** to preserve our season-long game caps while waiting for better situational alignments for the rest of our bench, including **Taylor Ward**, **Matt Chapman**, **Luis Arraez**, **Dylan Beavers**, **Jeremiah Jackson**, **Brendan Donovan**, **Zack Gelof**, **Ryan Waldschmidt**, and **Alex Freeland**. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** remains our most glaring absence due to his IL designation.",
